# Data flow diagrams (DFD) in Gane-Sarson notation: a recipe, to copy and
# adapt (pikslide --help dfd).
#
# The same order handling as the DeMarco/Yourdon recipe, in Gane-Sarson
# notation: only its processes and stores are drawn otherwise, with the
# macros of dfd.pik.
#
# THE SYMBOLS, AND WHAT DRAWS EACH
# process: a rounded rectangle, its number in a band at the top --
#   Take: dfdgsprocess("1", "Take" "order")
# data store: a rectangle open on its right, its id in a compartment --
#   Orders: dfdgsstore("D1", "Orders")
# external entity: a box -- Customer: dfdentity("Customer")
# data flow: an arrow, labeled with the data it carries -- arrow "order" above
#
# HOW IT'S LAID OUT
# - As in DeMarco/Yourdon: each flow labeled off the line, the top row
#   chained left to right, the second process hanging from the store it
#   reads and the second store beside it, and a bent flow drawn as a
#   labeled `line`, then an `arrow` on from where it ends.
# - A process is a block: its .n is the top of its number's band, its .w
#   and .e the middle of its sides, so that a flow from the side reaches
#   its name. Every process is one size, dfdprocwid by dfdprocht: break a
#   name into lines yourself, a string each ("Take" "order").
# - A store's .w is its closed end, at its id, and .e its open one; its .n
#   and .s are the middle of its lines.

include "dfd.pik"

Customer: dfdentity("Customer")
arrow "order" above right 150%
Take: dfdgsprocess("1", "Take" "order")
arrow "new order" above right 150%
Orders: dfdgsstore("D1", "Orders")

Ship: dfdgsprocess("2", "Ship" "order") with .n at 1in below Orders.s
arrow "order details" ljust from Orders.s to Ship.n

Inventory: dfdgsstore("D2", "Inventory") with .w at 1.2in right of Ship.e
arrow "stock level" above from Inventory.w to Ship.e

line "delivery note" above from Ship.w left until even with Customer
arrow up to Customer.s
